 "summary": "Embeddable command language by John Ousterhout in which every value is (conceptually) a string and the whole syntax is twelve substitution rules (the 'Dodekalogue'); control structures, namespaces, objects (TclOO) and coroutines are ordinary commands, and Tk made it a popular GUI scripting language.",
 "status": "active",
 "defined_by": [
  "reference-implementation"
 ],
 "file_extensions": [
  ".tcl",
  ".tm",
  ".tk"
 ],
 "homepage": "https://www.tcl-lang.org/",
 "influenced_by": [
  "lisp",
  "posix-sh",
  "c"
 ],
 "level_notes": {
  "0": "The C implementation (core.tcl-lang.org fossil repo, mirrored at github.com/tcltk/tcl) compiles scripts to bytecode (tclCompile.c) executed by tclExecute.c; Tcl_Obj dual representation (tclObj.c) caches non-string forms.",
  "1": "No external standard; the Tcl(n) man page with its twelve rules is the language definition, the other TclCmd man pages define the built-in commands, and changes go through TIPs (Tcl Improvement Proposals)."
 },
